tasks.descriptionMarkdown:
Add a `test-support` function in `gpui_platform` returning `CosmicTextSystem::new_without_system_fonts("IBM Plex Sans")` loaded with `gpux_fonts::ALL`. Make `gpui_wgpu` available to it on every desktop OS, including macOS.

- Add the `text = "bundled"` argument to `#[gpux::test]` in `crates/gpui_macros/src/test.rs`. It builds the context with `TestAppContext::build_with_text_system`. Keep the generated code resolvable for crates that depend on `gpux` and `gpux-platform`, as `bench.rs` already does.
- Write the test group `crates/gpui/tests/test_support_text.rs`:
  - exact advances and line heights for fixed ASCII strings in IBM Plex Sans and Lilex;
  - wrap points at a fixed width;
  - ellipsis truncation;
  - widths of multi-run styled text;
  - an unknown family falling back to a bundled font;
  - the default context still on `NoopTextSystem`.
- Record expected values on Windows. Lane A CI on Linux and macOS (A-2) proves they are OS-independent.

Replace the wall-clock reads listed in `d-clock` with `BackgroundExecutor::now()` reached through the app or window context:

- `gestures.rs` (`filter_at`, `handle_event_at` and `tick_momentum_at` callers, plus deadline checks);
- `scroll_activity.rs`;
- `animation.rs`, `animated_frame.rs`;
- `list.rs`, `div.rs` (scroll momentum, scrollbars);
- `editable_text/` (programmatic scroll);
- `img.rs` (loading timers).

Keep every production constant and behaviour.

Write the test group `crates/gpui/tests/deterministic_motion_clock.rs`. Using only `advance_clock`, `run_until_parked` and simulated frames, it asserts:
- long press fires at its threshold and not before;
- touch fling decays to a deterministic rest offset;
- smooth programmatic scroll reaches its target at its duration, and a wheel cancels it;
- `with_animation` progress at fixed times;
- reduced motion bypass;
- identical results for the same seed.

gpui has no scrollbar timer or repeat state machine of its own (it publishes thumb offsets in the input turn; scrollbar chrome is consumer-owned), so there is no scrollbar-repeat case to cover.

**Input simulation.** Add to `crates/gpui/src/app/test_context.rs`:
- IME composition helpers through the focused input handler (marked-text replace and update, commit, cancel);
- `simulate_key_up`;
- click with an explicit click count;
- `simulate_drag` along a path with a held button and modifiers.

All of them go through `Window::dispatch_event` or the input handler, exactly as platforms do.

**Recorded platform calls.** In `TestWindow`/`TestPlatform`, replace `unimplemented!()` (minimize, zoom, show-window-menu, hide, reveal-path, run) with recorded calls readable from tests.

**Window options.** Add test window options for logical size, scale factor and display size; the defaults are unchanged.

**Test group:** `crates/gpui/tests/input_simulation_completeness.rs`.

Record the port as the gpux skill requires:
- commit bodies name Zed #64718 and #64842 with their merge commits and the gpux adaptations;
- `docs/history/zed-imports.tsv` and `docs/history/upstream-provenance.md` record the port;
- the `update-from-zed-gpui-closure.md` patch-surface checklist gains an entry: the headless renderer is gpux-adapted, supports Windows, and must be preserved on imports.

Create `crates/gpux_conformance` in publishable shape:
- package `gpux-conformance`, `[lib] name = "gpux_conformance"`, workspace metadata, a `description`, Apache-2.0;
- a workspace member, plus a dependency key `gpux_conformance = { package = "gpux-conformance", ... }`;
- a row in the README crate table;
- `exclude` for `tests/goldens/**`.

Add the modules `probe`, `compare` and `report`, the latter defining the `gpux-conformance: PASS|FAIL|SKIP <scene> <detail>` line protocol.

**Test groups:**
- `tests/image_compare.rs`: relative probes, tolerance and differing-pixel ratio, backend-named goldens, `GPUX_UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS`, failure artifacts under `artifacts-temp/testkit/`.
- `tests/golden_policy.rs`: 32 KiB cap, naming, orphans, package exclusion.

Implement the following in `gpux_conformance`:
- `SceneSpec`: id, areas, size, scales, lanes, build, script, expectations;
- the `Script` steps from `d-catalog`;
- the `Host` trait, with `TestHost` (test platform, bundled text, executor clock, optional headless renderer);
- at least 30 deterministic scenes covering every area listed in SCENE-1.

Scenes use only bundled fonts and the executor clock. Their expectations encode current behaviour.

**Test group:** `tests/catalog_coverage.rs`. It checks area coverage, unique ids and complete metadata, and keeps the catalog compiling for `wasm32-unknown-unknown` without default features.

Write `tests/lane_a.rs`, which runs every lane-A scene through `TestHost`. For each scene it covers:
- layout bounds with bundled text;
- scripted interactions through semantic locators: click, drag, wheel, touch, type, IME, keys;
- state assertions: focus, selection, scroll offsets, drag-and-drop results;
- gestures and animations under `advance_clock`;
- devtools semantics and the accessibility tree.

The suite must not need a GPU or display.

Update `.github/workflows`:
- pin `ubuntu-24.04`, `windows-2025` and `macos-15`;
- turn the existing `test` job into a matrix over the three OSes;
- add `lane-a` on the three OSes;
- add `lane-c`:
  - on `ubuntu-24.04`: install `mesa-vulkan-drivers` and set `VK_DRIVER_FILES` to the lavapipe ICD found at runtime;
  - on `windows-2025`: set `GPUX_WGPU_ADAPTER=software`;
  - on `macos-15`;
- upload `artifacts-temp/testkit/**` on failure.

macOS entries stay disabled until `macos-only-metal-port-and-ci-repair` lands (M-2 enables them).

Commit the `lavapipe` goldens produced by the first CI run's artifacts. Pushing a branch for the CI run needs the owner's approval.

Create `tooling/testkit` in publishable shape: package `gpux-testkit`, bin `gpux-testkit`, workspace member, README row.

**Commands.** `lane <ids…>` with `--list`, `--scene` and `--grep`:
- lanes a and c delegate to the conformance test targets;
- the other lanes are wired in later sprints and fail clearly until then.

**Behaviour:**
- prerequisite checks with actionable errors;
- Windows `.cmd` shim resolution;
- parsing of conformance result lines;
- the final summary line and exit code;
- artifacts under `artifacts-temp/testkit/<lane>/`.

**Tests:** the crate's own tests, including fixture-based parsing of stdout, logcat and Playwright list-reporter output.

Add the `desktop_host` example to `gpux_conformance`, running scenes through an `AppHost` on `gpui_platform::application()`. Then wire `gpux-testkit lane d` to launch it per scene batch and parse its results.

**Coverage:**
- window open and present;
- `render_to_image` probes on the real surface;
- resize and scale-factor changes;
- clipboard round trip;
- close without panics;
- OS input:
  - X11 (Xvfb) through XTest/`xdotool`;
  - Windows through `SendInput`;
  - macOS through in-app injection.

**Local proof.** Prove it on Windows. Also run under the WSL clone with `xvfb-run` (X11) and WSLg (Wayland), and record what works.

**The `web_host` example** (cdylib):
- initializes through `gpui_platform::web_init`;
- picks the scene and backend from the URL;
- exposes `globalThis.__gpuxConformance`: `ready`, `backend`, `locate(query)` returning CSS-pixel bounds, and `probes`.

**`gpux-testkit web build [--verify]`:**
- nightly `-Z build-std=std,panic_abort` with atomics and shared-memory flags;
- the wasm-bindgen CLI version taken from `Cargo.lock`;
- generated `index.html`;
- verification of shared memory, the `wasm_thread_entry_point` export and the explicit constructor path.

**`gpux-testkit web serve`:** COOP/COEP/CORP headers and the SPA fallback.

Neither command uses RayX.

Create `tools-node/` with `package.json`, `pnpm-lock.yaml`, `playwright.config.ts` and `fixtures/`.

**Profiles:**
- `ci-linux-swiftshader`: headless, one `--enable-features=Vulkan,CDPScreenshotNewSurface`, `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --use-angle=swiftshader --use-vulkan=swiftshader`;
- `local-gpu`: `channel: 'chromium'` new headless, or `--headed`;
- `windows-software`: `--use-angle=d3d11-warp --enable-unsafe-webgpu`.

**Fixtures:**
- backend assertion (WebGPU unless forced WebGL2);
- `crossOriginIsolated`;
- console and panic failures with an explicit allow-list.

**Specs:** `@canary` specs.

**Testkit wiring.** `gpux-testkit web e2e --profile --grep` starts `web serve`, runs Playwright through pnpm and maps the results to the summary line.

Write tagged specs (`@pr`/`@dpr2`, plus `@timing` for lane G) against conformance scenes, using trusted CDP input at located bounds and screenshot probes:
- `backend` (forced WebGL2, stubbed `navigator.gpu`);
- `viewport` (DPR 1/2, ResizeObserver);
- `window` (frame loop, close cleanup);
- `pointer`;
- `wheel` (all delta modes);
- `keyboard`;
- `touch`;
- `ime` (`Input.imeSetComposition`);
- `clipboard`;
- `html-element`;
- `html-island` (opt-in);
- `text-fallback`, which takes over `examples/hello_web/test_canvas_fallback.mjs`;
- `threads`, which takes over `test_queue.mjs`;
- `assets` (SVG through `Svg::external_path`);
- `cursor`;
- `lifecycle` (focus, blur, visibility);
- `viewport-safe-area`;
- a Dawn-validation smoke that renders every pipeline and fails on any WebGPU validation error.

Delete the migrated `.mjs` scripts once their specs pass.

Add a `lane-b` workflow on `ubuntu-24.04`:
- **build job:** a pinned nightly with `rust-src` and wasm32, wasm-bindgen from `Cargo.lock`, then `gpux-testkit web build --verify`, uploading the dist;
- **sharded e2e jobs:** Playwright `--with-deps` install and the `ci-linux-swiftshader` profile, uploading blob reports;
- **report job:** merges the blob reports.

PRs run `@canary` and `@pr` at DPR 1; a nightly schedule runs everything except `@timing`. Pushing a branch for the CI run needs the owner's approval.

Add `gpux-testkit android run [--serial]`:
- `adb install` and `am start`;
- parse logcat result lines;
- drive touch with `adb shell input tap/swipe`, including a pan and a fling;
- pause/resume through home/`am` to assert surface loss and recovery;
- record the backend actually selected (Vulkan, or the GL fallback);
- pull PNGs into `artifacts-temp/testkit/e/`.

Prove it on an available emulator or USB device.

Add a `lane-e` job on `ubuntu-24.04`:
- KVM enabled through udev rules;
- `reactivecircus/android-emulator-runner` with an x86_64 image;
- `gpux-testkit android build` and `android run`;
- logcat and PNG artifacts uploaded.

It runs nightly, and on PRs that change `crates/gpui_mobile/`, `crates/gpui_wgpu/`, `crates/gpux_conformance/` or `tooling/android/`. If KVM is unavailable, record the blocker and the local-device fallback in `docs/testing.md`.

Add `gpux-testkit lane g`. It refuses to run under `CI` and runs:
- lane B in the `local-gpu` profile (optionally `--headed`), including `@timing` and `@dpr2`;
- lanes C and D on the hardware adapter with relative probes only;
- `cargo bench -p gpux-platform --bench randomized_element_tree` and the `#[gpux::bench]` suites.

It writes a timing summary under `artifacts-temp/testkit/g/`. Prove it with a local run on the Windows DX12 machine.

Write `docs/testing.md`, covering:
- the lane table;
- commands and prerequisites per host;
- CI cadence;
- the golden update flow;
- `GPUX_WGPU_ADAPTER`, `GPUX_UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS` and `VK_DRIVER_FILES`;
- the verified Chromium flags and troubleshooting.

Rewrite these to route validation through the lanes and `gpux-testkit`, keeping RayX only as a consumer with the API-pairing rule:
- `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md`: Validation, Consumers and the AgentX note;
- `.claude/skills/gpux/SKILL.md`;
- the skill references `multithreaded-wasm-apps.md`, `installation-and-usage.md`, `macos-linux-wasm-prerequisites.md`, `windows-prerequisites.md`, `sticky-positioning.md`, `update-from-zed-pr.md`, `update-from-zed-gpui-closure.md`, `update-from-wgpu.md` and `update-from-gpui-mobile.md`;
- `docs/installation-and-usage.md`: remove the RayX/.NET notes.

In `LINUX-WSL.md`, add the gpux lane commands for WSL and mark the RayX sections consumer-only.

Update the rules and state of these memory domains:
- `repository/validation`: lanes, headless CI with the backend assertion, local real-GPU proof, testkit commands;
- `renderer` and `renderer/wgpu`: WebGPU and forced-WebGL2 proof through lane B, plus the headless renderer;
- `gpui`: the `diagnostics` feature name;
- `platform/web`: lane B instead of headed counters;
- `platform/mobile`: lanes E/F instead of RayX's xtask;
- `platform/desktop`: macOS proof through lanes C/D;
- `repository/agentx-memory`: no RayX runtime-proof repository.

Prepare the epic's decisions for promotion, and keep the indexes consistent.

**`gpux-testkit ios run --simulator`:**
- boots a pinned Simulator;
- runs `simctl install` and `launch --console-pty`, and parses the result lines;
- copies PNGs from `simctl get_app_container`;
- asserts lifecycle, Metal-through-wgpu startup, CoreText metrics within tolerance, and in-app touch;
- reports pixel steps as SKIP with the reason when the hosted Simulator lacks Metal.

**CI.** Add a `lane-f` job on `macos-15`, running nightly and on path-filtered PRs.

**Docs.** Document the local iPhone flow (signing team, `xcrun devicectl`) in `docs/testing.md`, and add the iOS facts to `platform/mobile` memory.
